  "url": "homework-6.html",
  "type": "Worksheet",
  "number": "",
  "title": "Homework 05",
  "body": " Homework 05    The purpose of the homework is to:     Practice applying the concepts and techniques discussed this week    Reveal gaps in your understanding so you know what to study    Enable a productive re-read of the textbook chapter\/engagement with other course resources.     The homework is both a valuable learning opportunity and a gauge of how well you're doing. Did you solve all the problems by yourself with no help? Incredible! If that didn't happen, that's OK: we're not expected to get everything right the first time. What you need to do is look carefully at what you needed help with, and then make a study plan, which will likely include solving more problems, to fill in the gaps in your understanding.      Instructions: Complete all the exercises below and submit your work by the due date.     K&K Mass Flow Problems      4.9    4.10    4.11    4.22    4.23    5.10 Note: you don't need energy arguments here as long as you understand the basic motion of the chain while it falls. Momentum is all it takes.        Wing-Flapping Frequency of Birds  A hovering animal (like a hummingbird) must use its wings to generate a lift force that is equal in magnitude to its weight. It does this by flapping its wings and pushing air downwards. This process can be complicated, but our goal is to obtain a simple estimate of how often a bird must flap its wings: this will be a frequency f (beats per second)    Find an approximate formula for what this lift force should depend on. Clearly define any parameters you introduce, such as the length of the wings. At this point, its fine to keep your lift force in terms of a speed instead of trying to introduce the frequency.    Now figure out how to relate, approximately, the speed you used in the previous part to the wing beating frequency (hint: frequency has units of 1\/time)    Let us now make the gross estimate that all animals have roughly the same density . Using your assumption, find how the frequency f scales with animal size: that is, you should find an expression of the type . The constant isn't particularly important (it can depend on lots of things that we aren't taking into account here!), but the power n can be compared to experiment. This is an example of an allometric scaling relation       "
