  "body": " Homework 06    The purpose of the homework is to:     Practice applying the concepts and techniques discussed this week    Reveal gaps in your understanding so you know what to study    Enable a productive re-read of the textbook chapter\/engagement with other course resources.     The homework is both a valuable learning opportunity and a gauge of how well you're doing. Did you solve all the problems by yourself with no help? Incredible! If that didn't happen, that's OK: we're not expected to get everything right the first time. What you need to do is look carefully at what you needed help with, and then make a study plan, which will likely include solving more problems, to fill in the gaps in your understanding.      Instructions: Complete all the exercises below and submit your work by the due date.     K&K Problems     5.11 Note: you can break up the motion into parts, but you can also directly use the results of section 5.2 here, but there are some subtleties to be discussed next week.    5.9    5.13 and 6.1 (they are really a unit)    5.14 and 6.2 (also a unit)       Energy of two particles in the CM frame  Show that the kinetic energy of a two-particle system, , where is the speed of the center of mass; is the relative speed between the two particles, and . Hint: start by solving for in terms of . You may work in one dimension instead of using vectors, but this result is valid in 3D.    Conservation of Energy-Like Problems in Other Contexts  Whenever you see a differential equation of the type , even if mechanics doesn't have anything to do with it, you can apply conservation of energy to say that is constant and use energy-diagram methods to learn about your solution! One such example is the Friedmann equation, which describes the expansion of the universe using a scale factor a(t) that describes how length scales increase with time     The Friedmann equation for a universe with just matter and dark energy can be written, after some simplification, as . are positive constants, related, respectively, to the amount of matter and dark energy in the universe. Find the potential energy  for this problem    Sketch a graph of the potential energy. Note: the particular values of aren't super important here, all that matters is the shape of the graph.    The total energy  in the Friedmann equation can be positive, negative, or zero. Assuming that the universe starts from a small size (so at some point in time), use your energy diagram to describe the possible fates of the universe, and take some time to ponder that it is possible to do so much with very little math (once you have the Friedmann equation!).       "
