  "body": " Homework 07    The purpose of the homework is to:     Practice applying the concepts and techniques discussed this week    Reveal gaps in your understanding so you know what to study    Enable a productive re-read of the textbook chapter\/engagement with other course resources.     The homework is both a valuable learning opportunity and a gauge of how well you're doing. Did you solve all the problems by yourself with no help? Incredible! If that didn't happen, that's OK: we're not expected to get everything right the first time. What you need to do is look carefully at what you needed help with, and then make a study plan, which will likely include solving more problems, to fill in the gaps in your understanding.      Instructions: Complete all the exercises below and submit your work by the due date.     K&K Problems  For each problem, be clear if you are using real work or Center of mass work in your solution.     5.1    5.2    5.3    5.4    5.6    5.8    5.18       The Astro-blaster  A tennis ball with a small mass m sits on top of a basketball with a large mass M (see figure). The bottom of the basketball is a height above the ground, and the tennis ball is standing right on top of it. After the balls are dropped, how high does the tennis ball go? Assume and that the balls are separated by a very small distance, so that they basically collide the moment the basketball reverses its motion. No need to solve the elastic collision problem again from scracth. If you're up to the challenge, imagine what would happen if you have N balls stacked on top of each other, all satisfying [this is optional].   A small ball is on top of a large ball. The large ball is at a certain height above the ground.       The Rocket-Powered Skateboard  Consider a rocket-powered skateboard that is about to roll downhill. To escape on the right with the maximum speed, where should it fire a brief impulse (i.e., blast its engines)?   A rocket on level ground ahead of a depression that ends at a hill.   A rocket is on level ground ahead of a depression to its right. The depression leads to a hill at higher altitude than the one the rocket starts.  The lowest point at the depression is at heigth below the top of the hill, and the rocket starts at height , which is less than , below the hill.    At first glance, it is not obvious why the timing of the impulse would even matter. To have a concrete model for the impulse, let's say that the rocket, of mass , throws a blob of mass backwards, which ends up moving with speed relative to the rocket.  To avoid any ambiguity: immediately after the blob ejection, someone standing on the skateboard would see the blob traveling away with speed .     Show that the change in the rocket's velocity immediately after the blast is the same regardless of where you fire the blast.    Show that the total change in kinetic energy (rocket + fuel) also does not depend on where you fire the blast.    Finally, show that the change in kinetic energy of the rocket does depend on where the blast is fired    To make sure you're interpreting everything correctly, complete the following sentence: The total kinetic energy of the system increases by . Mechanistically (or shall we say chemically?), this extra energy comes from , so it makes sense that it's a fixed amount regardless of when the rocket fires. However, the resulting increase in rocket kinetic energy can be different, because .        "
