  "body": " Explicit Solution to the twin paradox  The following problem is based on an explicit numerical example of the twin paradox due to Sir Charles Darwin (grandson of the biologist). On New Year's Day, 3000, an astronaut (A) sets out from Earth at speed 0.8c and travels to the nearest star, -Centauri, which is just about 4 light-years away as measured in the Earth frame of reference. Having reached the star she immediately turns around and returns to Earth at the same speed, arriving home on New Year's Day, 3010, by Earth time. The astronaut has a brother (B) who remains on earth, and they agree to send one another greetings by radar-telephone on every New Year's Day until the traveler gets back.   Show, briefly, that A sends only 6 messages (including the one made on the last day of her trip), whereas B sends 10.    Draw a space-time diagram of A's journey as plotted in the Earth reference frame. Draw also the world lines of all the radar signals that B transmits. Verify with the help of the diagram that A (the astronaut) has received only 1 signal up to the moment of her turnaround and receives the other 9 during the return half of the trip.    Draw another space-time diagram, again in the Earth reference frame, showing the world lines of the astronaut and of all the signals that she sends. Verify that her brother receives one message each 3 years of Earth time for the first 9 years after the departure, and then receives 3 more messages during the last year, making a total of 6—which is just right, since for the astronaut the trip has taken 3 years each way.     "
