  "title": "Reading Quiz 02: Newton’s 1st and 2nd laws",
  "body": " Reading Quiz 02: Newton's 1st and 2nd laws    Contents: This quiz covers sections 2.1 through 2.6.   Instructions: Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload it as a pdf using the File Upload question below. When you submit the quiz, don't navigate away from Canvas. Scroll down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.      The result of example 1, relating to seems to depend on the fact that Commander Wright is ahead of Commander Earhart. Draw a diagram with Earhart ahead of Wright (or Wright ahead of the fleeing ship!) and show that you get the same relationship (be careful with signs!).      You and a friend are on a road trip, with your friend driving. As your friend takes a sharp turn to the left, you feel yourself thrown to the right. Give an example of an inertial reference system you might want to use to analyze your motion. Identify the forces that act on you and draw a diagram representing them.      Look at the figure accompanying problem 2.2 of K&K. This figure is slightly misleading in terms of choosing an intertial reference frame. Explain how you might change the figure or what statements you might add to make the figure physically more clear. Hint: is there an inertial coordinate system that can use the distance from the block to the pulley as a coordinate?      A mass is tied to a string attached to the ceiling and going around a horizontal circle as shown in the figure below (Link to a video of the motion) . If you were to draw a diagram showing the relevant forces to analyze the motion of the mass, which forces must be included? Your answer can just be the letters that apply.      String tension.    Gravitational force.    Ceiling reaction force.    Air resistance.    Centripetal force.    Centrifugal force.      A ball tied to a string going on a circle. The top of the string is attached to the ceiling.   The top end of a string is attached to the ceiling, and the bottom end is attached to a ball. The string is titled, and the ball moves in a circle along a horizontal plane, not changing its height.        "
