  "body": " Reading Quiz 13: Rotational Kinematics and Angular Momentum    Contents: This quiz covers sections 1.4.2, 1.6 (components of cross products), section 8.2 (up to example 8.3), and sections 7.1 and 7.2. You should do the reading roughly in this order.   Instructions: Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload it as a pdf using the File Upload question below. When you submit the quiz, don't navigate away from Canvas. Scroll down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.      Draw a picture showing the equivalence of the corkscrew rule to the following method to find the direction of : put the index finger of your right hand in the direction of , then have your middle finger point in the direction of . The cross-product then points in the direction of your thumb.      The definition of angular velocity in terms of derivatives of angles as shown in K&K is nice but not really correct (you’ll fix this in 4011). For now, show that for all the examples in 8.2 you can find the direction of in the following way. Curl your right hand in the direction of rotation, and then points along your thumb. Its magnitude is given by the instantaneous about the axis of rotation (this part is a definition, don't show it).      Check that for either choice of origin in example 7.2, the relationship . Use the rule from the above problem to find Lesson: if a vector obeys , then that vector is rotating with constant angular velocity about the axis determined by .      Verify, using the right hand rule (draw some pictures!) that the directions of in example 7.2 are correct.      It may seem weird that in the previous example has a horizontal component but think about the following questions.   If you didn't know the particle was in a conical pendulum, and all you had was a picture showing you , could you say whether the particle was rotating along the z axis or the x axis?    In which sense, then, does the direction of  splits the difference or, rather, accounts for both possibilities?        "
