  "title": "Reading Quiz 20: Nuclear Binding Energy and Reactions",
  "body": " Reading Quiz 20: Nuclear Binding Energy and Reactions    Contents: Reading covers sections 8.6 through 8.8.   Instructions: Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload it as a pdf using the File Upload question below. When you submit the quiz, don't navigate away from Canvas. Scroll down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.      Explain why the graph in page 238 shows that iron is more stable than both helium and uranium.      In atomic mass units, the masses of proton, neutron, and helium (2 protons and 2 neutrons) are, respectively, . To answer the questions below, you will need the conversion factors and .    How much energy does it take to break apart a helium nucleus? By this I mean the energy to take the helium nucleus and turn it into 2 protons and 2 neutrons that are infinitely far away from each other, with no kinetic energy. This is known as the binding energy  of helium.    What is the ratio of the binding energy of helium to its rest energy?    The mass of the hydrogen atom is u, and its binding energy is eV. What is the ratio of the binding energy of hydrogen to its rest energy?    Using your calculations from this question, explain why conservation of mass holds in chemistry but not in nuclear physics.       "
